# Adversarial Gate — Cursor

Before doing anything else: read `core/GATE.md` in this project and follow
it. It has the rules, the review protocol, the cost discipline and the
calibration reconciliation. Nothing here duplicates it — this file only adds
what's specific to Cursor.

## Execution Model (Cursor)

Cursor runs **one agent per chat thread**. There is no built-in orchestration
comparable to Claude Code's subagents or agent teams — no native way to spawn
several agents that report back into the same context automatically.

- For work that needs genuine parallelism, use multiple **Background Agents**
  (async runs against the same repo) and synthesise their results yourself in
  a follow-up conversation. Justify each Background Agent you spin up the
  same way `core/GATE.md` §1.3 asks you to justify any agent — there is no
  free lunch just because the primitive exists.
- For everything else, treat `core/GATE.md`'s agent design as a set of
  sequential passes by this one agent. Say so explicitly in STEP 2 — don't
  present sequential passes as if they ran independently.

**Isolated self-critique (§2.2):** Cursor has no separately addressable
subagent for this. The nearest honest equivalent is a **new chat**, given
only the plan (not the reasoning that produced it) and asked to attack it.
Disclose that this isolation is weaker than a true separate context — a new
chat in the same project can still see prior chat history depending on
Cursor's settings; check before relying on it.

## Activation

This is an `.mdc` rule with `alwaysApply: false`, i.e. **Agent Requested**
mode — Cursor decides whether to attach it based on the `description` above,
the same way Claude Code auto-triggers on a skill's description. To force it,
`@`-mention the rule by name. Cursor has no native `/` custom-command system
for rules as of this writing — don't promise one to the user.
